{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i -C 2 'aristotle|bicommutant|closeout|stabilizer_hyperfinite' <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | head -160 && sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,260p' AGENTS.md && sed -n '1,260p' aristotle_tasks/P40-bicommutant-normal-transport.md && sed -n '1,220p' PROGRESS.md && sed -n '1,220p' CONTEXT-LEDGER.md && sed -n '1,220p' SOURCE-CONTRACT.md\"","aggregated_output":"120-- symptom: the L² foundation is described as operator Pauli relations, finite/tail factorization, quasilocal closure, global state/GNS, corner trace/fullness, or the Overleaf theorem -> fix: retain these as explicit open obligations. [Task 2]\n121-\n122:# Task Group: lean/stabilizer standalone Lean tensor-product and trace formalization\n123-\n124-scope: separate, approval-gated Lean audit/formalization of the proposed stabilizer-region tensor-product theorem, including checked algebraic transport and an explicit analytic-theorem stop boundary\n125:applies_to: cwd=lean/stabilizer (source context=source-workspace); reuse_rule=reuse the checked algebraic modules and analytic-obligation checklist only after rechecking pinned mathlib and source assumptions\n126-\n127-## Task 1: audit the manuscript target and formalize the algebraic layer\n--\n129-### rollout_summary_files\n130-\n131:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T19-10-40-cRYC-standalone_lean_stabilizer_hyperfinite_formalization_blocked.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T21-10-40-019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T03:04:54+00:00, thread_id=019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2, partial: algebraic results checked; full theorem blocked)\n132-\n133-### keywords\n--\n139-### rollout_summary_files\n140-\n141:- rollout_summaries/2026-08-02T19-10-40-cRYC-standalone_lean_stabilizer_hyperfinite_formalization_blocked.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/rollout-2026-08-02T21-10-40-019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-03T03:04:54+00:00, thread_id=019fc3e2-abbe-71f2-9c08-db9f0ba1e6f2, blocked: source and library lack required analytic inputs)\n142-\n143-### keywords\n144-\n145:- gLU, refinement maps, Pauli multiplier, C*-completion, product-state factorization, quasi-equivalence, bicommutant, normal trace, hyperfinite factor classification, PROGRESS.md\n146-\n147-## User preferences\n--\n156-- Checked modules: `Formalization/TraceTransport.lean` proves `StabilizerHyperfinite.isUnitalTracial_pullback`; `CStarEquivExtension.lean` establishes continuity/isometry only for supplied complex C*-algebra `StarAlgEquiv`s; `AlgebraicLimitStarHom.lean`/`AlgebraicLimitTraceTransport.lean` are algebraic direct limits; `CoherentPhaseQubitState.lean` proves the selected-Z phase-weighted expectation from coherent finite-stage data. [Task 1]\n157-- `lake build`, focused module builds, axiom audits, and two-pass `pdflatex` for `blueprints/001-tensor-product-trace.tex` passed. There were no `sorry`, `admit`, or user axioms in checked sources. [Task 1]\n158:- Missing inputs remain coherent finite-stage gLU/refinement maps, Pauli multiplier/involution and phase calibration, quasi-local C*-completion, product-state factorization, normal/spatial GNS-bicommutant identification, and hyperfinite-factor classification. [Task 2]\n159-\n160-## Failures and how to do differently\n--\n1362-- The bootstrap contract that proved reusable is: create exactly `project_graph.json`, `project_graph.schema.json`, and `validate_project_graph.py`; refuse overwrites; optionally seed one `Investigation` node only when the user explicitly names the initial workline [Task 1]\n1363-- A local validator is worth keeping even when the generic helper exists. The useful check bundle here was schema version/type validation, duplicate-ID checks, dangling-edge checks, legal relation-endpoint checks, and `locator.path` existence or anchor audits where locators are present [Task 1][Task 2][Task 3][Task 4]\n1364:- For repo-root graph work, the quickest structural check is `python3 <original-home>/.codex/skills/project-graph-scheme/scripts/project_graph.py validate --graph project_graph.json`; closeout-quality verification then adds `python3 -m json.tool` on the JSON files, `jsonschema.validate(...)` or `jsonschema -i`, and locator audits when nodes point into real files [Task 2][Task 3][Task 4]\n1365-- The `vsn` rollout established a durable repo-governance pattern: put the human maintenance contract in root `AGENTS.md` plus `PROJECT_GRAPH.md`, keep `project_graph.schema.json` as the typed contract, and use `validate_project_graph.py` as the local enforcement point that all agents can rerun [Task 4]\n1366-- The `lennarts_problem` workspace confirmed that non-git TeX/transcript folders still benefit from the same typed graph stack; the graph should point to local artifacts like `conversation_transcript.md`, `geometric_mean_inequality_conversation.md`, `combined_problem_solution.tex`, and `combined_problem_solution.pdf` rather than trying to duplicate their mathematical content [Task 2]\n--\n1373-- symptom: a validator accepts malformed graphs or throws on bad structure -> cause: object-shape checks or schema-first failure handling are too permissive -> fix: reject malformed node/edge entries early and fail cleanly on schema errors before dereferencing `nodes`/`edges` [Task 1][Task 4]\n1374-- symptom: heading/anchor discovery commands fail during graph seeding -> cause: the regex is more complicated than the task requires -> fix: use simpler `rg` patterns when you only need section headings or labels for locator audits [Task 3]\n1375:- symptom: graph closeouts overstate mathematical certainty -> cause: the graph is treated as a proof artifact instead of a provenance/navigation layer -> fix: state explicitly that the graph records the current support status and does not independently re-prove the mathematics [Task 2][Task 3]\n1376-\n1377-# Task Group: source-workspace approval-gated local manuscript review\n--\n1454-### rollout_summary_files\n1455-\n1456:- rollout_summaries/2026-07-15T13-22-01-YKcF-general_type_ii_stabilizer_criterion_workline.md (cwd=source-workspace, rollout_path=<original-home>/.codex/sessions/2026/07/15/rollout-2026-07-15T15-22-01-019f65f1-0246-7c83-9c24-dd45127a4929.jsonl, updated_at=2026-07-15T13:44:33+00:00, thread_id=019f65f1-0246-7c83-9c24-dd45127a4929, same rollout; distinct routing value for compile/verification and conditional-closeout guidance)\n1457-\n1458-### keywords\n--\n1493-- symptom: a helper cannot reopen an existing file while updating a workline README in this sandbox -> cause: patch-helper reopening was unreliable in this environment -> fix: be ready to use another non-destructive edit path if the helper cannot reopen the target [Task 1]\n1494-- symptom: `technical.tex` fails on the first compile with an undefined macro -> cause: required math macros such as `\\\\cR` were not defined in the draft preamble -> fix: define custom macros before the first compile and rerun immediately on the exact error [Task 2]\n1495:- symptom: first-pass LaTeX warnings look like unresolved problems at closeout -> cause: references only settle after a second pass -> fix: rerun `pdflatex` and report final-state warnings separately from first-pass noise [Task 2]\n1496-- symptom: visual PDF inspection cannot be completed through the sandboxed image viewer -> cause: rendered PNG paths could not be reopened in this environment -> fix: treat `pdfinfo` plus successful PDF generation as the verified fallback and state that visual inspection remained partial [Task 2]\n1497-- symptom: `P_\\Lambda\\otimes I_{\\Lambda^c}` is called a normalized density matrix -> cause: it is a constraint projector whose trace is generally not one -> fix: for a finite density matrix use `P_F/rank(P_F)`; also ensure any `|S_F|` in a rank formula means the number of independent stabilizers (`log_2|G_F|`), not a redundant generator-list length [Task 3]\n--\n2072-- A realistic first Lean layer is finite qubit matrix/tensor products, Pauli trace orthogonality, finite abelian stabilizer groups, group-average `P_F`, rank/density formula, and partial-trace compatibility. Canonical-state existence/positivity may be assumed initially. [Task 1]\n2073-- Upstream mathlib has C*-algebra classes, spectra, continuous functional calculus, positivity/CP maps, and GNS (`PositiveLinearMap.GNS`, `gnsStarAlgHom`, `gnsNonUnitalStarAlgHom`); it lacks ready-made Pauli/stabilizer/partial-trace libraries. [Task 1]\n2074:- Treat the support-corner-to-semifiniteness argument as a separate frontier: strong/strong-* topology, bicommutants, Kaplansky density, ultraweak normality, finite projections/central support, and full-finite-projection-implies-semifinite are not an integrated finished package. Defer central direct-integral/factor classification. Suggested staged interfaces: `finite_stabilizer_density_and_partial_trace`, `support_monotonicity_from_compatible_projector_states`, `abstract_support_corner_is_tracial`, `full_finite_projection_implies_semifinite`. [Task 1]\n2075-\n2076-## Failures and how to do differently\n---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n# Manuscript-Only Context Policy\n\n## Source authority\n\nFor manuscript-facing statements, the sole mathematical source is\n`sources/manuscript/main.tex`.\nThe local summary and locator record is `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`. Do not modify\nthe manuscript from this project.\n\nEvery claim described as coming from the manuscript must identify an exact\nline range in `main.tex`. Repository history, earlier chat context, and other\nartifacts are not substitute sources.\n\n## Context firewall\n\nDo not introduce, assume, or attribute to the manuscript any gLU,\nrefinement, layer-coherence, or finite-coordinate construction unless it is\npresent in the cited manuscript lines or is explicitly introduced as a new\nauxiliary assumption. A Lean file compiling does not make its unstated\nphysical or operator-algebraic hypotheses part of the manuscript.\n\nClassify each item as one of:\n\n1. **manuscript-derived**: exact source locator and no stronger conclusion;\n2. **auxiliary assumption**: explicitly labelled and used only conditionally;\n3. **Lean infrastructure**: reusable formal mathematics that makes no source\n   claim; or\n4. **legacy exploratory work**: retained for reference but unavailable as\n   support for a manuscript-derived assertion.\n\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md` records the current legacy items.\n\n## Current formalization boundary\n\nThe target discussed in the manuscript is the short-range regional-algebra\nstatement at `main.tex:1740-1752`; its proposed proof is at\n`main.tex:1891-2004`. Keep separate the asserted algebraic identification,\nthe product-state/GNS steps, the unformalized operator-algebraic completion and\nspatial tensor-product steps, and the trace conclusion. A tracial state is\nappropriate only for the finite pure-sector case. The infinite pure-sector\ntarget instead calls for a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight when the\nrequired hypotheses and von Neumann infrastructure are supplied.\n\n## Aristotle task packets\n\nEvery new task under `aristotle_tasks/` must begin with exactly one context\ndeclaration:\n\n- `Source basis: main.tex:<line range>` for a manuscript-facing task; or\n- `Infrastructure only:` for reusable Lean material that makes no manuscript\n  claim.\n\nIt must then list every auxiliary assumption, state its exact Lean target, and\nsay whether its conclusion is conditional. A task may not cite a legacy\nartifact as its source basis. Use `aristotle_tasks/TEMPLATE.md` for new\npackets.\n\n## Verification and reporting\n\nReport Lean compilation separately from mathematical validation. For each\nmanuscript-facing milestone, state which source clauses are represented, which\nassumptions remain external, and which analytic/von Neumann-algebraic claims\nare not yet formalized in the pinned Mathlib version.\n# P40 — bicommutant and normal transport boundary\n\n## Context declaration\n\n- **Source basis:** `main.tex:1970-2004`\n\n## Mathematical target\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/BicommutantNormalTransport.lean`. Starting\nfrom P39's GNS unitary, use the checked bicommutant core to prove the algebraic\nconjugation formulas for generated bicommutants. Package the precise additional\nnormality/extension assumptions required to turn this into a normal star\nequivalence and to transport normal functionals or folia.\n\n## Boundary\n\nDo not invoke an unproved double-commutant theorem, quasi-equivalence theorem,\nor spatial tensor-product theorem. If pinned Mathlib lacks the analytic step,\nrecord its exact statement as an explicit interface, not an axiom.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n`lake build AristotleCandidates.BicommutantNormalTransport` succeeds with no\nplaceholder and clearly separates algebraic bicommutant facts from analytic\nclosure/normality facts.\n# Formalization progress\n\n## Context policy (2026-08-05)\n\nAll future manuscript-facing formalization takes its context only from\n`SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`, with exact locators in the manuscript `main.tex`. Earlier\ngLU/refinement/layer-coherence artifacts remain available only as explicitly\nlabelled legacy infrastructure; they are not manuscript hypotheses. See\n`AGENTS.md` and `CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`.\n\nThe manuscript-facing module sequence is now `Formalization.Manuscript`:\nsource boundary, finite model, algebraic limit, product state, conditional GNS\nbridge, trace dichotomy, and only then the pending theorem contract. Existing\nimplementation files remain in place and are imported through facades.\n\n- P17 — under a supplied finite adapted-coordinate/word realization, the\n  finite pure/mixed product state is exactly the stabilizer indicator on every\n  supplied source Pauli basis word.\n- P18 — a supplied finite matrix-factor tensor decomposition transports the\n  finite product trace to an algebraic tracial state with its elementary-tensor\n  formula.\n- P19 — a supplied C-star model plus compatible states packages the existing\n  GNS unitary and algebraic commutant transport into one bridge interface.\n- P23 — supplied coherent finite-stage star equivalences induce the infinite\n  algebraic direct-limit regional equivalence `alpha`, with finite-stage and\n  inverse formulas. No C-star or von Neumann extension is claimed.\n- P24 — compatible target-stage algebraic states pull back along `alpha` to a\n  unique algebraic direct-limit state with its exact finite-stage expectation\n  formula and conditional stabilizer-indicator corollary.\n- P25 — supplied C-star completions and an extension of `alpha` yield an\n  isometric continuous C-star equivalence and continuous state pullback;\n  existence of those completions remains an explicit assumption.\n\n## Checked Lean results\n\n- `TPT-001` / `TPT-002` — an algebraic tracial state pulls back along a\n  complex `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- TPT-003 — algebraic traciality is reflected as well as preserved by a\n  complex star-algebra equivalence; hence a nontracial product state cannot\n  become tracial after an algebraic coordinate identification.\n- `FMT-001` — the normalized trace on a nonempty finite full matrix algebra is\n  an algebraic tracial state.  Positivity is proved by the explicit\n  trace-of-a-star-square sum-of-squares identity.\n- `FSC-001` — a finite corner equipped with a star isomorphism to a full matrix\n  algebra inherits that algebraic tracial state.\n- `FTT-001` — the finite algebraic tensor product of two matrix factors is\n  identified by the Kronecker star isomorphism, and its normalized trace\n  factorizes on elementary tensors; tensoring with a matrix identity preserves\n  the trace.\n- `FAM-001` — if `R` has an algebraic tracial state, then its finite matrix\n  amplification `Matrix n n R` has the normalized diagonal tracial state.\n  Positivity is checked as a finite sum of coefficient-algebra star-squares.\n  An explicit `StarAlgEquiv` transports this finite type-I product trace to a\n  target algebra.\n- `TMT-001` — the finite type-I product is now identified canonically, not\n  supplied as an assumption: `Matrix n n ℂ ⊗[ℂ] R` is star-isomorphic to\n  `Matrix n n R`.  Its trace factors on elementary tensors as the normalized\n  matrix trace times the coefficient trace.\n- `DTT-001` — the actual dyadic finite-stage system is constructed as\n  `A₀ = ℂ`, `Aₙ₊₁ = Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) Aₙ`, with diagonal identity-qubit\n  embeddings.  Its normalized traces are compatible with every stage map,\n  and their algebraic directed limit carries the induced algebraic tracial\n  state.\n- FPU-001 — for every nonempty finite matrix factor, its algebraic tensor\n  product with the concrete dyadic algebraic limit carries the checked product\n  tracial state.  The theorem includes the elementary-tensor formula\n  tr_p(a) times qubitUHFTrace(u).\n- FPU-002 — any supplied regional `StarAlgEquiv` into that finite-pure\n  product model induces an algebraic tracial state on the regional algebra,\n  with the checked formula on inverse images of elementary tensors.\n- `AIT-001` — compatible unital cyclic finite-stage functionals glue to a\n  unital cyclic linear functional on mathlib's algebraic directed direct\n  limit.\n- `AIT-002` — compatible stagewise star-algebra maps induce an involutive,\n  additive, multiplication-reversing star on that algebraic direct limit.\n  Compatible algebraic tracial states then glue to an ordinary algebraic\n  tracial state there.\n- `SPC-001` — an adapted Hamel basis identifies the label space with finitely\n  supported X/Z coordinate labels and maps the singleton