{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && sed -n '1970,2004p' sources/manuscript/main.tex && printf '\\\\nLIVE-TARGET-EXISTS\\\\n' && test -e AristotleCandidates/BicommutantNormalTransport.lean && echo yes || echo no && printf '\\\\nIMPORT/DECLARATION-CROSSCHECK\\\\n' && rg -n 'theorem gnsConj_range|def gnsConj|theorem gnsConj_sourceGNSRepresentation|def gnsUnitary|def gnsConjStarAlgEquiv|image_centralizer_of_mulEquiv' AristotleCandidates/{StatePreservingGNSModel,CStarAlphaGNSBridge,BoundedStateGNS}.lean && printf '\\\\nALL-SUSPICIOUS-FORMS\\\\n' && rg -n '\\\\b(sorry|admit|axiom|opaque|unsafe|implemented_by|native_decide)\\\\b' aristotle_responses/P40-extracted/aristotle-stabilizer_hyperfinite-hpzu5w6c_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/BicommutantNormalTransport.lean || true\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' <original-home>/.codex/adversarial_review.md && sed -n '1970,2004p' sources/manuscript/main.tex && printf '\\\\nLIVE-TARGET-EXISTS\\\\n' && test -e AristotleCandidates/BicommutantNormalTransport.lean && echo yes || echo no && printf '\\\\nIMPORT/DECLARATION-CROSSCHECK\\\\n' && rg -n 'theorem gnsConj_range|def gnsConj|theorem gnsConj_sourceGNSRepresentation|def gnsUnitary|def gnsConjStarAlgEquiv|image_centralizer_of_mulEquiv' AristotleCandidates/{StatePreservingGNSModel,CStarAlphaGNSBridge,BoundedStateGNS}.lean && printf '\\\\nALL-SUSPICIOUS-FORMS\\\\n' && rg -n '\\\\b(sorry|admit|axiom|opaque|unsafe|implemented_by|native_decide)\\\\b' aristotle_responses/P40-extracted/aristotle-stabilizer_hyperfinite-hpzu5w6c_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/BicommutantNormalTransport.lean || true\"","aggregated_output":"# Adversarial Review Protocol\n\nUse this before closing substantive Codex work. The goal is to find defects, not\nto summarize accomplishments.\n\n## Scope\n\n1. Identify the artifact types touched: `code`, `math`, `tex`, `computation`,\n   `source-backed`, `workflow`.\n2. Inspect the diff or new artifact against the user's request.\n3. Report findings by severity first. If there are no findings, say so and state\n   the remaining risk.\n\n## Universal Checks\n\n- Did the work answer the actual latest user request?\n- Did it modify unrelated files or broaden scope?\n- Did it overwrite, reformat, or revert pre-existing user changes?\n- Are generated files, caches, logs, plots, PDFs, or build artifacts handled\n  intentionally?\n- Are final claims stronger than the evidence supports?\n- Are verification commands actually relevant to the changed artifact?\n\n## Code Checks\n\n- Does the changed behavior match the stated interface and edge cases?\n- Are tests missing for a changed behavior, bug fix, parser, numerical routine,\n  CLI command, or user-facing workflow?\n- Are errors handled where inputs, files, network calls, or external tools can\n  fail?\n- Is there hidden global state, path sensitivity, nondeterminism, race risk, or\n  stale cache behavior?\n- Did dependency, config, or environment changes alter unrelated workflows?\n\n## Math Checks\n\n- Are all assumptions, domains, signs, conventions, normalizations, and boundary\n  conditions stated before use?\n- Are definitions used consistently throughout the derivation?\n- Is any implication actually reversible, or was a one-way statement used both\n  ways?\n- Are there division-by-zero, invertibility, regularity, convergence, branch-cut,\n  gauge, or boundary-term assumptions?\n- Do limiting cases, dimensions/units, symmetries, and known special cases agree?\n- Are symbolic substitutions justified rather than smuggling in the result?\n- Are claims labeled correctly as proof, derivation, check, conjecture, or\n  numerical evidence?\n\n## TeX And Source-Backed Checks\n\n- Does the document compile if compilation was expected?\n- Are labels, references, citations, bibliography entries, and figure paths valid?\n- Are source-backed claims tied to exact locators when required?\n- Is quoted or paraphrased material clearly separated from the agent's synthesis?\n- Does the exposition define terms before using them in load-bearing statements?\n\n## Computation Checks\n\n- Is the computation reproducible from a recorded command?\n- Are inputs, random seeds, versions, tolerances, and units documented when they\n  affect the result?\n- Are outputs interpreted correctly and not over-promoted beyond the computation?\n- Were independent sanity checks used where feasible: small cases, limiting\n  cases, dimensional analysis, symbolic identities, or alternate algorithms?\n\n## Closeout Template\n\nUse this structure internally, then surface the concise version to the user:\n\n```text\nFindings:\n- [severity] file/path:line - issue and consequence.\n\nChecks run:\n- command -> result.\n\nResidual risk:\n- what was not checked, or why the artifact is not a proof/complete validation.\n\nCleanup:\n- remaining dirty/untracked files and whether they are intentional.\n```\n    We now need to show that this algebra is isomorphic to the local algebra\n    $\\pi_\\w(\\cA_\\G)''$.\n    Theorem III.5.1.6 of \\cite{Blackadar} states that the existence of an algebraic\n    $\\ast$-isomorphism between the generated von Neumann algebras\n    is equivalent to quasi equivalence of the\n    representations.\n    Proposition 2.12 in \\cite{TakesakiI} shows that the representations are\n    quasi equivalent, if the closed invariant subspaces of $\\cP^*_\\G$\n    associated to the representations agree. These spaces are the restrictions\n    of the elements of the predual $(\\pi_{\\w}(\\cP_\\G)'')_*$ to $\\cP_\\G$ (c.f.\n    below Def 2.10 in \\cite{TakesakiI}) and form closed linear subspaces of\n    $\\cP^*_\\G$. We therefore have to show that these subspaces agree for both\n    representations. We can do so by recognizing that for a von Neumann algebra\n    $\\cM$ represented on a Hilbert space $\\cH$, a normal functional $\\P$ can be\n    written as (c.f. Theorem III.2.1.4 in \\cite{Blackadar}) \n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\P(a) = \\sum_n \\mel{\\x_n}{a}{\\e_n}\n    \\end{equation}\n    with $\\x_n,\\e_n \\in \\cH$ and \n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\sum_n \\norm{\\x_n} < \\infty,\\quad \\sum_n \\norm{\\e_n} < \\infty.\n    \\end{equation}\n    So that, if we can recover the state induced on $\\cP_I$ \\LS{do we need to\n    recover them only of $\\cP_\\G$? Do we also need to show that the norms\nagree, so the sums are finite in both Hilbert spaces?} by any vector of each Hilbert\n    space, then we know that the two closed subspaces agree.\n\\end{proof}\n   The quasi equivalence is proven by showing that all normal states of\n   $\\pi_\\w(A)''$ are also contained in the predual (normal states of)\n   $\\pi_{\\tilde{\\w}}(\\cA)''$ by explicitly constructing them in the latter (i.e. one can\n   write an explicit vector in the GNS representation). One can then show, that\n   if the linear folia of two representations (normal states of the induced von\n   Neumann algebra restricted to the original algebra) agree, that the\n   representations are quasi-equivalent. The first sentence implies that the\n   linear folia agree, thus they are quasi equivalent.\n\nLIVE-TARGET-EXISTS\nno\n\nIMPORT/DECLARATION-CROSSCHECK\nAristotleCandidates/StatePreservingGNSModel.lean:452:noncomputable def gnsUnitary : M.sourceGNS ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] M.targetGNS :=\nAristotleCandidates/StatePreservingGNSModel.lean:476:noncomputable def gnsConj (T : M.sourceGNS →L[ℂ] M.sourceGNS) :\nAristotleCandidates/StatePreservingGNSModel.lean:482:noncomputable def gnsConjStarAlgEquiv :\nAristotleCandidates/StatePreservingGNSModel.lean:489:theorem gnsConj_sourceGNSRepresentation (a : M.toAlphaGNSData.SourceCStar) :\nAristotleCandidates/StatePreservingGNSModel.lean:494:theorem gnsConj_range :\nAristotleCandidates/CStarAlphaGNSBridge.lean:200:noncomputable def gnsUnitary : A.sourceGNS ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] A.targetGNS :=\nAristotleCandidates/CStarAlphaGNSBridge.lean:228:noncomputable def gnsConj (T : A.sourceGNS →L[ℂ] A.sourceGNS) :\nAristotleCandidates/CStarAlphaGNSBridge.lean:234:noncomputable def gnsConjStarAlgEquiv :\nAristotleCandidates/CStarAlphaGNSBridge.lean:244:theorem gnsConj_sourceGNSRepresentation (a : A.SourceCStar) :\nAristotleCandidates/CStarAlphaGNSBridge.lean:251:theorem gnsConj_range :\n\nALL-SUSPICIOUS-FORMS\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
